<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1874 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ko e fasi ʻo e tuʻi ʻo e ʻOtu Tonga</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Tonga, a song for the king first used in 1874, with words by a Tongan prince.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Uelingatoni Ngū Tupoumalohi</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Carl Gustav Schmitt</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Kingdom of Tonga</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tongan</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Tonga</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first used in 1874</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Prince Uelingatoni Ngū Tupoumalohi, first used in 1874)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ko-e-fasi-o-e-tui-o-e-otu-tonga">Ko e fasi ʻo e tuʻi ʻo e ʻOtu Tonga</a>&quot; is the national anthem of Tonga. Its title means &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the King of the Tonga Islands&quot;; when the Tongan monarch is a woman it is sung as &quot;Ko e fasi ʻo e kuini ʻo e ʻOtu Tonga&quot;, &quot;Song of the Queen of the Tonga Islands&quot;. In everyday life it is better known as &quot;Fasi fakafonua&quot;, the &quot;National Song&quot;.</p><p>The words were written by the Tongan prince Uelingatoni Ngū Tupoumalohi and the music by Carl Gustav Schmitt, a German-born composer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. The anthem was first used in 1874. Its music is printed both in Western notation and in tuʻungafasi, the Tongan system of music notation.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A royal anthem: it addresses the monarch of the Tonga Islands, and its title changes with whether a king or a queen reigns. The mood is solemn and loyal.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Tongan</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Tongan</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="to">ʻE ʻOtua māfimafi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Almighty God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="to">Ko homau ʻeiki koe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are our Lord,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="to">Ko koe ko e falalaʻanga</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are the one we trust in,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="to">Mo e ʻofa ki Tonga;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the love for Tonga.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="to">ʻAfio hifo ʻemau lotu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Look down on our prayer</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="to">ʻAia ʻoku mau faí ni</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>which we are now making,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="to">Mo ke tali homau loto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and answer our hearts' wishes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="to">ʻO maluʻi ʻa Tupou.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and protect Tupou.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Prince Uelingatoni Ngū Tupoumalohi (1874); the Tongan text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ko_e_fasi_%CA%BBo_e_tu%CA%BBi_%CA%BBo_e_%CA%BBOtu_Tonga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>line 1.3: 'falalaʻanga', the place or ground of trust, refuge.</li><li>line 1.4: 'Mo e ʻofa ki Tonga', literally 'and the love towards Tonga': God is the one who loves Tonga.</li><li>line 1.5: 'ʻafio hifo', 'look down, be present below', the honorific verb used of God and the king.</li><li>line 1.7: 'tali homau loto', 'answer (receive) our hearts', i.e. our wishes.</li><li>line 1.8: Tupou, the royal line (King George Tupou I when the anthem was written).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
